unicate  with one another  and that there are no common data  formats so the participants proposed as  next step to create some sessions uh on  the different industry standards and  their practical  applications uh and this is what we did  we created a series of webinars where we  discussed about the adoption of open  standards and why multiple standards are  necessary conditions for digitalization  uh we then saw the need of mapping all  the process communication and data  standards that are being used by the lab  of the future community  in an easy to understand format  so now what you see on the right side  the standards mapping across the r d  value chain this is a current initiative  and we are gathering ideas on how this  mapping should look like  it can either be an infographic type or  it can be a decisional tree that will  help the user to figure out which  standard is relevant for their work and  for their lab environment  now some standards exist and we can do  the mapping of all of them but others  need to be created where there are gaps  and this can be done in two ways  one way is from bottom up  this  for example coming from the research  need of the organization to have some  communication or process or data  standards to fit the purpose or it can  be developed from top down which means  from the compliance from the regulatory  side  and as an example of an industry-wise  initiative developed by the  alliance and its members we have the  health notation which is the  hierarchical editing language for  macromolecules  which is a system of standardized naming  for complex biomolecules  it's widely adopted it's used for  representing therapeutic large molecule  in the bioregistration systems  and helm is now an fda recognized  submission format which also received an  award of the best practice from the  bioid world  here is another example of initiative  that can help standardize the data  across the industry this is called the  idmp ontology project the idmp comes  from identification of medicinal  products and the terminology is being  used in different domains as you can see  on the columns that are marked in blue  in the development in the production in  regulation authorization and so on  the project is aiming at creating the  ontology that will line top down the  idmp iso standards to the in-house  standards of the organizations to reduce  the current implementation  inconsistencies at the organization  level and in this way it can save time  and costs  on the right side you can see another  example of a regulatory initiative which  is called in vitro pharmacology project  where the fda  invited the pistol alliance to help  develop the data standards for in vitro  pharmacology bio assay  the project just started and the aim is  to accelerate the submission process  by defining the ontologies that will  enable better data reuse interpretation  and understanding  and all these examples and top-down  standards that are being developed will  be seen at the laboratory level in a  period of time but from a process  perspective these top-down standards are  very efficient because because they  created the need of everybody to accept  the same ontologies and data standards  now the pistol alliance  has  also other communities of interest that  are connected to the lab of the future  and we create also collaborations and  partnership between these communities  on the left side you can see the data  governance community this is managed by  my colleague john rice  in the middle you see the aiml community  of interest  this is managed by my colleague vladimir  makarov and on the right side you see an  ad another example of community which is  the uix community managed by my  colleague paola de matas  so if we take one by one with the data  guy finance community we created the  series of standards webinars data  governance is essential to enable  digital transformation and to advance  innovation and this growing community  already has more than 30 companies that  bring together  opinion leaders to identify best  practices common obstacles and some  potential solutions that we can all work  together  the ai community of interest is aiming  at discovering and promoting the best  ways to use these  new promising technologies  to apply them to solve the current  challenges and to educate  the companies through presentations and  discussions  and when implementing tools and software  ai ml an important aspect is to consider  the change management and particularly  the user adoption  so the dedicated user experience  community which is formed by  practitioners from the pharmaceutical  health care and software industries  is delivering frameworks and guides and  toolkits to enable the businesses to  adopt  ux principles and methods and their most  recent initiative is to create concepts  and ideas that will help the lab of the  future community  last few years we saw rapid growth in  terms of the number of new  pre-competitive collaborations between  the pharma companies and ai providers  and software providers  and in general the uh the organizations  from the r d equal system  and in 2022 digital transformation  continues to accelerate  and the pharmaceutical industry is  seeing the benefits of non-competitive  collaborations and at the beginning  when the companies are learning how to  assess their organization and the  potential for digital transformations  we do encourage them to join different  non-competitive platforms and groups to  learn from the experience of the other  companies and same for the organizations  that are  the digital journey because it's useful  to contribute into and to help each  other by sharing the use cases and best  practices and lessons learned and in  this way the organizations together can  share both the risks and the benefits of  the deliverables  now if you are not part of an  organization yet  if you are a student or an early  graduate i do encourage you to join the  communities of interest  most of the discussions are open you can  join without any registration cost and  you can be more in contact with what is  happening in the in the industry  i would say a recommendation to  stay informed to subscribe to different  newsletters about your area of interest  to join different webinars and  discussions  and very important to be proactive uh so  for example if you see a challenge uh  discuss it with others maybe look for  some solutions and if you see a  challenge and a solution  you can join different working groups um  you can reach out to different  not-for-profit organizations that have  the means and the contacts uh to  validate the concept and to bring the  right stakeholders at the table  i want to show you how at the pistoia  alliance we create new projects we are  constantly developing uh new projects uh  and the process starts with the ideation  phase  uh where the pre-competitive project  idea is being raised the idea can come  from anybody  then the idea is being validated with  the community in terms of the benefits  and the interest  and once there is a buy-in for that  particular idea  it then starts to take a shape into a  project that has a clear problem  statement and the industry benefit  the outcome can be a member-funded  project or a program it can be a new  formed community of experts  and  this is the exact same path  that  we went on for creating the woman in  stem pilot program that i'm about to  present you  so firstly why is the women in stem  topic important for so many  organizations  you might not see  gender imbalance at the university or in  your early career levels  but the gender imbalance in the top  executive roles is a well-documented  case unfortunately so student so studies  showed  that women are more likely to leave the  stem career pipeline than men  leading to a wider gap  at the senior level  so women account for only three percent  of the ceos in this field and this is  why the pistol alliance has chosen to  focus this pilot's scheme on the  leadership roles because we believe that  um we believe in the top-down  changes and in creating more role models  for the future generations  and this was the process  we started in april last year at the  pistol alliance conference where we had  the women in stem panel with some very  interesting discussions that continued  even after the end of the conference  and by the way some of the most  interesting discussions happened before  and after the presentations so if you're  a student or an early graduate at the  conference  don't limit uh to  to the presentations to listening to the  presentations try to network and join  the conversations that happen during the  breaks  or in the organized networking events  and the poster sessions and and so on  you can get a lot of valuable  information from there  and connect with the right people  so um  going back to  how we started this program uh in april  last year we received the feedback from  the participants that really made us  think um  of the different programs that we can  initiate to help the woman who the women  who want to start uh or grow into their  stem career  so in quarter three we gathered more  interested parties and we asked the  participants what do they think are the  biggest barriers for women in stem  and the lack of child care maternity  policies  depending on  also the locations but also the absence  of female role models were mentioned as  the barriers  but by far the greatest thing to be the  workplace culture  and we didn't go into more depth at that  time  but we knew that the workplace culture  is highly influenced by the leadership  and the strategic organizational  directions and the management  so what was also interesting  in q3 was the feedback that came from  one of the only two men  who joined the court uh he experienced  for the first time the feeling of being  in the minority  uh and this feedback confirmed that in  the women in stem program all genders  need to be involved we agreed that any  diversity effort needs to incorporate  the intersectionality  which is the acknowledgement that  everyone has their own unique experience  when it comes to discrimination and that  gender can make people feel marginalized  and that is why we wanted to make sure  that everybody is well represented and  that men are included in all aspects of  the program  in q4 we started the different  ideas on how the program should look  like and the most appealing  idea to our members  was the one to create a cross-company  training and networking program which is  what we are piloting now with top  managers from the pharma companies  uh the current pilot program uh brings  together 40 participants from 12  countries from three continents for a  period of six months  we started in january we will finish in  june this year we currently have 17  companies involved in this pilot stage  and each group of 10 participants  includes  two men as minority  uh the group meetings are held weekly we  have accredited trainer and coach and we  also have monthly networking sessions  uh each group includes diversity in  terms of the gender race location role  function  almost half of the participants are from  the us the other half is mostly from  europe and china and the prestai  alliance brought together  leaders with different areas of  expertise because we wanted to see  different perspectives  and these are limited but not included  are including but not limited to the  drugs research development data science  industry regulation drug safety and so  on  and just to tell you that these women in  stem pilot program it's a first step in  a wider diversity and inclusion in stem  leadership initiative  and we are aiming to run a series of  future programs as of september this  year  uh using the feedback and the learnings  from this pilot program um  we would like to form a community of  leaders who share the same values and  that want to contribute to the change in  the overall industry either by directly  changing or by promoting uh quality in  their organizations  if you want to find out more about  the pistol alliance and how you can get  engaged please do reach out to us on the  website and again for students and early  graduates uh join the industry  discussions uh stay informed ask  questions and be proactive  reach out to us  thank you very much  thank you so much and for such a  wonderful presentation i have a question  how do competing big pharma companies  collaborate in the pistol alliance  projects  well we facilitate a non-competitive  environment so for example we bring  together uh  projects or ideas or solutions that if  the companies are working together  there's a benefit for the whole industry  there is no competitive advantage right  so that's why we can bring different  companies that even if for example in  the in the day-to-day business  environment they are in competition  we bring them together with projects and  initiatives that all of them can benefit  because imagine that if for example a  company has a big challenge  uh one thing is to try to solve the  challenge by its own  which means investing resources and  resources can be hiring a lot of people  to try to solve that issue uh  spending the money the time and so on or  they can share the risk right and the  resources with other companies that are  facing similar challenges and try to  solve them uh together  okay we got it  yeah thank you for answering the  question and the next question is uh  what is the challenge that big pharma is  trying to solve with bioinformatics  well i think it's also related with the  with the data standards and and actually  when it comes to challenges from the  industry we always ask the participants  and um  we want to understand what is the  challenge and what we can do for that  challenge right  thank you and do we have any more  questions  okay we have one more question what are  some of the challenges faced in the  current role as a strategic theme lead  and how do you handle those challenges  well the challenges that i see are  mostly related with uh ensuring that we  have all the right stakeholders at the  table so for example  if somebody comes to me with an idea or  a challenge and says look i need more  people that  would be willing to work  together on solving this issue right for  me one of the challenges is okay uh i do  have a lot of contacts who is the best  person to come and work on this  particular project all validating that a  challenge that one company is facing  it's actually a challenge that more  companies are facing because many times  it happens that the company comes with a  challenge  that is not very sure if other companies  are facing similar one so then this this  comes the validation process that i'm  doing going and talking with different  companies seeing if that challenge is  similar and if we have an idea on a  solution that we can work  together to solve it  okay  okay  and uh our last question  uh mohammed is asking how can we join  women in stem program  so currently the women in stem is for  the  people that are in the top executive  roles in the pharma because the way we  started this program we saw that at the  entry level so it depends on where you  are right if if you are at the entry  level in a career there are  similar programs for entry level right  um what we saw we saw the need of doing  the same in the top executive to make  sure that the people that join at the  entry level  can have the  same chances for advancing the career  right so uh our program is targeted at  leadership roles and you can join by by  uh going on the website of the pistol  alliance and you will see there under  the project the program and you can  register either to join either to be  informed or with the newsletter  information  okay perfect i hope maham you have your  answer and you can join 